Now I need to add `apply_style` free function (VISIBLE/OPACITY/WIDTH/HEIGHT/CONTENT_MARGINS + CSS for background/border/font). And composite handling in sync_node + sync_composite_children. And `Picker` options (children → dropdown string list — the picker_widget currently ignores options; I'll populate in update via a DropDown string list).

Let me add `apply_style` free function near apply_padding. It applies:
- VISIBLE → set_visible
- OPACITY → set_opacity
- WIDTH/HEIGHT → set_size_request (FILL/HUG sentinels → use -1 for natural? Gtk set_size_request with -1 = natural. FILL → we don't set fixed size; use -1. HUG → -1 too. Actual pixel → set. So: if v > 0 → set_size_request(w, current_h). Keep simple: only positive values set size request.)
- CONTENT_MARGINS → margins (uniform).
- BACKGROUND_COLOR / BORDER_* / FONT_FAMILY / FONT_WEIGHT via CSS provider — this needs the renderer to hold a provider, but apply_style is a free fn without renderer state. To keep it simple, I'll implement background/border via a module-level per-widget CSS: create a CssProvider per widget, register on its style_context. But creating a new provider every update() is wasteful; only create when the CSS changed. Simpler: build the CSS string, create a fresh provider each time, add to style_context. That re-adds providers (leak-ish but GTK handles). For correctness and simplicity, I'll apply background/border/font-family/font-weight via a CSS provider created lazily and registered on the widget's style_context. Since update() is called on every frame for every node, creating a provider each time is heavy. Better: only build when the relevant properties are present. For a POC renderer this is acceptable.

Actually — let me keep `apply_style` minimal and reliable: handle VISIBLE, OPACITY, WIDTH/HEIGHT (positive only), CONTENT_MARGINS via direct widget calls; and BACKGROUND_COLOR + BORDER_WIDTH/COLOR/RADIUS + FONT_FAMILY/WEIGHT via a CSS provider registered on the widget's style_context, built from a `style_css(node)` string. Let me write it.

```rust
fn apply_style(widget: &gtk::Widget, node: &HostNode) {
    // Visibility / opacity / sizing (direct widget properties).
    if let Some(bits) = node.properties.get(&property_id::VISIBLE) {
        widget.set_visible(*bits != 0);
    }
    if let Some(bits) = node.properties.get(&property_id::OPACITY) {
        widget.set_opacity(f64::from(f32::from_bits(*bits)));
    }
    let w = node.properties.get(&property_id::WIDTH).map(|b| f32::from_bits(*b));
    let h = node.properties.get(&property_id::HEIGHT).map(|b| f32::from_bits(*b));
    if w.is_some() || h.is_some() {
        let w = w.filter(|v| *v > 0.0).map(|v| v as i32).unwrap_or(-1);
        let h = h.filter(|v| *v > 0.0).map(|v| v as i32).unwrap_or(-1);
        widget.set_size_request(w, h);
    }
    if let Some(bits) = node.properties.get(&property_id::CONTENT_MARGINS) {
        let m = f32::from_bits(*bits) as i32;
        widget.set_margin_top(m);
        widget.set_margin_end(m);
        widget.set_margin_bottom(m);
        widget.set_margin_start(m);
    }
    // CSS-backed decoration (background, border, fonts).
    let css = style_css(node);
    if !css.is_empty() {
        let provider = gtk::CssProvider::new();
        provider.load_from_string(&css);
        widget.style_context().add_provider(&provider, gtk::STYLE_PROVIDER_PRIORITY_APPLICATION);
    }
}
```

`style_css(node)` builds `#<id> { ... }`? No — I need a selector. Without setting a widget name, I can't target the widget. Options: use `widget.set_widget_name(&format!("pathland-{}", node.id))` (widgets already have a unique id in the map) and CSS `#pathland-<id> { ... }`. GTK widget name is used for CSS id selector. So set widget name once (in apply_style or build), then CSS `#pathland-<id> { background-color:...; border:...; font-family:...; }`. But set_widget_name changes the GTK style class lookups (named widgets lose default styling? No, widget name is separate from classes; setting a name is fine).

Let me implement:
```rust
fn style_css(node: &HostNode) -> String {
    let mut css = String::new();
    if let Some(bits) = node.properties.get(&property_id::BACKGROUND_COLOR) {
        let (r,g,b,a) = unpack_color(*bits);
        css.push_str(&format!("background-color:rgba({:.0},{:.0},{:.0},{:.3});", r*255.0, g*255.0, b*255.0, a));
    }
    if let Some(bits) = node.properties.get(&property_id::BORDER_WIDTH) {
        let w = f32::from_bits(*bits);
        let (r,g,b,a) = unpack_color(node.u32_property(property_id::BORDER_COLOR, 0xFF00_0000));
        css.push_str(&format!("border:{w}px solid rgba({:.0},{:.0},{:.0},{:.3});", r*255.0,g*255.0,b*255.0,a));
    }
    if let Some(bits) = node.properties.get(&property_id::BORDER_RADIUS) {
        let r = f32::from_bits(*bits);
        if r != 0.0 { css.push_str(&format!("border-radius:{r}px;")); }
    }
    if let Some(family) = node.string_property(property_id::FONT_FAMILY) {
        css.push_str(&format!("font-family:'{}';", family.replace('\'', "\\'")));
    }
    css
}
```

And apply_style sets the widget name + provider:
```rust
let css = style_css(node);
if !css.is_empty() {
    widget.set_widget_name(&format!("pathland-{}", node.id));
    let provider = gtk::CssProvider::new();
    if let Err(e) = provider.load_from_string(&format!("#pathland-{} {{ {css} }}", node.id)) {
        eprintln!("css error: {e}");
    }
    widget.style_context().add_provider(&provider, gtk::STYLE_PROVIDER_PRIORITY_APPLICATION);
