**Toggle.java**:
```java
package com.pathland.view;

import com.pathland.view.emit.PathlandNode;
import com.pathland.view.signal.WritableSignal;

public final class Toggle implements View {
    private final ToggleStyle style;
    private final boolean selected;
    private final String label;
    private final WritableSignal<Boolean> binding;

    public Toggle(ToggleStyle style, boolean selected, WritableSignal<Boolean> binding, String label) {
        this.style = style;
        this.selected = selected;
        this.binding = binding;
        this.label = label;
    }

    public Toggle(boolean selected, WritableSignal<Boolean> binding) {
        this(ToggleStyle.SWITCH, selected, binding, null);
    }

    @Override
    public PathlandNode render(Environment env) {
        PathlandNode node = new PathlandNode(Components.TOGGLE);
        node.properties.put(Properties.TOGGLE_STYLE, (float) style.wire());
        node.properties.put(Properties.SELECTED, selected ? 1 : 0);
        node.propertyBindings.put(Properties.SELECTED, binding);
        if (label != null) {
            node.text = label;
        }
        node.valueInput = v -> binding.set(v > 0f);
        return node;
    }
}
```

Wait — propertyBindings SELECTED with a WritableSignal<Boolean>: ReactiveValues.from(SELECTED, signal) returns Boolean → 1/0. ValueTypes SELECTED = U8. Good. But binding is WritableSignal<Boolean> — the emitter effect reads signal.get() = Boolean → ReactiveValues.from converts. Good.

But there's a subtlety: node.properties SELECTED initial = int 1/0. The binding effect compares `Objects.equals(value, node.properties.get(SELECTED))` where value = 1/0 Integer. Initial effect run reads binding.get() = Boolean true/false → converts to 1/0 → equals initial → no opcode. Good.

valueInput = v -> binding.set(v > 0f). When host sends VALUE_CHANGED with value 1.0f → binding.set(true). Then binding effect fires → SELECTED re-emit. Good.

